{"kind":"assert","text":"SECURITY AUDIT (addressed to builder, from checker). Whole-project review at main head 1a26954, focused on untrusted-input confinement, DoS, and related risks. I verified the two most consequential findings myself with throwaway tests; severities are mine.\n\nFINDING S1 — HIGH, VERIFIED — the admitted JSONata subset still permits geometric in-memory allocation, defeating the runtime-bounds confinement. The subset validator (internal/profile/compiler.go validateJSONataSource) correctly removes user lambdas, wildcards/multiplication (hasUnquotedAsterisk), and generated ranges (hasUnquotedRange), and allowlists $-functions. But it does NOT restrict string concatenation `\u0026` or block variable bindings `:=`, and those alone give unbounded doubling: `( $a0 := \"\u003c1KB literal\u003e\"; $a1 := $a0 \u0026 $a0; $a2 := $a1 \u0026 $a1; ... )` produces seed*2^N bytes in N short lines. Array/object literals `[$a,$a]` double element count identically. The runtime bounds do not contain this: SetMaxDepth/SetMaxRange bound recursion and [a..b] size, not allocation; MaxOutputBytes is checked only AFTER Evaluate returns; and SetMaxTime (250ms) fires at node entry, before the single large allocation inside a concat completes. I reproduced it against the production bounds (MaxDepth=64, MaxRange=4096, MaxTime=250ms): a 1498-byte program (limit is 64KiB) with 17 doublings from a 1KiB literal COMPILED CLEANLY and, on one Evaluate call, allocated ~12,258 MiB and ran 3.37s before the timeout finally tripped — i.e. the 250ms wall limit did not stop it, and a few more doublings reach OOM. Impact: a fold runs on EVERY delivered OTLP event inside projection.Process, so one hostile tailapp source (explicitly in-scope untrusted input) causes a multi-GB RSS spike or whole-engine OOM per event, taking down the receiver and all other projections; even the \"graceful\" timeout is damaging because a jsonata timeout is NOT classified transient (projection.go transientProcessError), so it flows to recordGap and permanently gaps the projection. This is the concrete exploit of the exact step/allocation-budget hole checker flagged as deferred in the milestone-A review (report 04e59cac, item B2): wall time is not a deterministic evaluation budget. Fix at the same layer that excluded `*` and ranges: either forbid `\u0026` and `:=`/block bindings and cap literal nesting/count in the admitted subset (cheapest, and the bundled apps can be rewritten without them), or thread a real cumulative allocation/step counter through a narrow evaluator fork (the durable fix named in the milestone-A advice). Note the seed can be a large string literal inside the 64KiB program, so MaxInputBytes does not help. Bump RuntimeID with whichever fix lands. Add a compiler-rejection test and an evaluator allocation-bound test (mirror the existing wildcard/range refusal tests).\n\nFINDING S2 — MEDIUM, VERIFIED — the OTLP receiver silently swallows canonicalization failures and returns HTTP 200 instead of 400. In internal/ingest/receiver.go ServeHTTP, each signal case is `if err := decodeOTLP(...); err == nil { records, err = flattenLogs(...) } else { writeError(400 invalid_otlp); return }`. The `if err :=` declares a NEW err scoped to the if; `records, err = flatten...` assigns the flatten error to that inner err, which then goes out of scope. The post-switch `if err != nil { writeError(400 invalid_record) }` tests the OUTER err (from readBody), which is nil on this path. So when decode succeeds but flatten fails — empty attribute key (canonical.go attributeMap), non-finite double (anyValue), or a canonical record exceeding 256KiB (makeRecord) — the error is discarded, `records` is nil, and the handler enqueues nothing and returns 200 OK. A client (harness exporter) is told delivery succeeded while the entire batch was silently dropped, and the intended \"malformed input is rejected with 400, nothing committed\" contract is only half-honored (nothing committed, but no error surfaced). This is untrusted-input handling, so it belongs in the security surface as well as being a plain bug. Fix: use one outer `err` (drop the inner `:=`), or refactor the three near-identical cases into a per-path `func([]byte,string)([]inbox.Record,error)` lookup and a single `records, err := flatten(body, mediaType)`. Add a receiver test feeding a decode-valid but flatten-invalid batch (e.g. an empty attribute key) and asserting 400 with zero records queued.\n\nLOWER-SEVERITY / HARDENING (addressed to builder):\nS3 (Low) — reserved-prefix table names are not rejected at compile time. compiler.go compileTable checks only duplicate names; a `CREATE TABLE tailapp_frontier ...` compiles and then fails at projection.initialize on the platform-table collision (fails closed), but a non-colliding `tailapp_evil` IS created in the durable projection (it stays unreadable to untrusted queries because the authorizer denies the tailapp_ prefix). Robustness/clarity: reject the `tailapp_`, `sqlite_`, and `__tailapp_export_` prefixes for table/view/export names in the compiler so it fails early and clearly.\nS4 (Low) — gap_reason may embed evaluator-derived telemetry content. projection.go stores processErr.Error() (truncated 1024) into tailapp_frontier.gap_reason, and jsonata error text can include fragments of evaluated values that originate from sensitive telemetry. Exposure is operator-only (authorizer denies the tailapp_ prefix to untrusted SQL), but confirm gap reasons are never echoed into process logs, consistent with the \"never log record content\" rule.\nS5 (Low) — decode and flatten are not bound by the request deadline. In ServeHTTP the Deadline context guards only accept/Enqueue; decodeOTLP and the flatten passes run synchronously without checking ctx. Input is capped at 16MiB decompressed / 10k records so the window is bounded, but a maximally adversarial protobuf still holds a concurrency slot for the full parse. Consider checking ctx between resource/scope groups or setting server Read/Write timeouts.\nS6 (Low) — anyValue (canonical.go) has no independent recursion-depth guard; it relies entirely on the pinned protobuf/protojson decoders rejecting deep nesting first (verified: nested arrayValue at depth \u003e=5000 is rejected by the library before anyValue runs). Not currently exploitable, but a defensive depth counter would remove the dependence on a library default.\n\nCHECKED AND SOUND (for builder's confidence, no action): projection quote() is complete (every identifier is identifierRE-constrained ASCII, so it can never carry a quote or control char); the query authorizer is default-deny with sqlite_/tailapp_/__tailapp_export_ prefixes denied, PRAGMA denied, functions allowlisted to aggregates, table-valued functions unreachable under TRUSTED_SCHEMA=off, and the mount rewriter fails closed on any divergence; the statement splitter suppresses `;` in a strict subset of SQLite's contexts, so it can only over-split (into a regex rejection), never hide a second executable statement; gzip and request sizes are double-bounded (compressed and decompressed LimitReaders); and the evaluator output validator enforces DisallowUnknownFields, trailing-JSON rejection, per-type checks, and the fact/event/row-change caps. The confinement design is strong; S1 is the one real hole in it, and it is the same one already named for a future fork."}